| v279 (Claude, dasham/vitti refusal) | 80 DIFFER programs, every one run | the refusal of `vitti f = 3.5` | 79 same output and exit code; 1 changed (the program the fix was for) | **Landed.** Recorded in the changelog archive as "80 DIFFER programs, every one run: 79 print the same bytes with the same exit code." |
| R101A, v284 (Option A regions) | ELF 86 DIFFER, PE 0 DIFFER, Wine 0 DIFFER | every `kosh` program (Linux-only region) | 85 same; 1 changed (`examples/288_r86_kosh_alias_moves.sm`: old `a[0]=41 k[0]=99`, new `a[0]=99 k[0]=99` — the alias fix working) | **Landed as v284.** |
| R104 FIXALL, v286 | ELF 551 DIFFER (0 byte-identical), PE 0 DIFFER | every ELF program (the Linux-only prologue stack guard, `E_GAHANATA`) | all 551 same output and exit code, 0 changed | **Landed as v286.** The set was "every ELF program" — the largest DIFFER in the tree's history, and it landed because the set was stated and the outputs were all the same. |
| R107 / R108 / R115 (v287–v295) | 1 DIFFER each | one program newly refused (`examples/287_r82_kosh_after_mandapa.sm`, `examples/316_r107_kosh_kept_by_a_prakriya.sm`, `examples/329_r113_sankhya_bindu.sm` — all "source changed", old rc=0 new rc=1) | 0 same, 1 changed each (a refusal is a behaviour change, and it was the intended one) | **Landed.** |
| R129, v305 (slice S2 fix) | 84 DIFFER, ELF and PE | the slice programs | all 84 same output and exit, 0 changed | **Landed as v305.** (The same file also records the v304 step: 1 DIFFER, `examples/121`: refused -> prints 1.) |
| R175 step 2, v320 (this round) | 38 DIFFER | exactly the programs with a block | 37 same; 1 changed (`blind_kosh_via_call.sm`, 99 -> 0) | **Not landed.** Staged on `r176-block-exit-zero`, commit message "NOT READY - a decision". |

Two things the table shows. First, the 38 is not the first non-zero guard — it is the seventh such case in the tree's history, and the smallest of the large ones (38 against 248, 551, 86, 84, 80). Second, the treatment has been uniform: a non-zero DIFFER is never, on its own, a reason to revert. What decides it is always the accounting — the set stated, every program run old and new, every output change explained. The v263 case is the closest parallel to the 38th: there, too, one program's output changed, and it was accepted because the program was already wrong ("a bug in the example, exposed not caused"). Here the parallel is one step removed: the 38th program was not already wrong in the same sense — it pins a hole that is still open — but its output change is the fix working on a dangling read, and the pin's purpose is preserved by the update. That is the justification §2.4 gives, and it is the same *shape* of justification as v263 and v284 (the output change is the intended fix, stated per program), not a new kind of excuse.

**Counts in this section, re-derived, not carried:** guard corpus 783; programs mentioning `mandapa` 75 (73 ASCII + 2 Devanagari-only); in the differ-list 38 (37 ASCII + 1 Devanagari मण्डप); the other 37 are refused identically (31) or comment-only (6); v319 guard 587 identical / 196 refused / 0 DIFFER; step-2 run 549 identical / 196 refused / 38 DIFFER, 0 new; differ-outputs 37 same output, 1 OUTPUT DIFFERS.

