## 3. Task 3 — the book, checked

**Verdict: all three passages are TRUE as far as they go, and all three share one imprecision of scope — and the reference passage has one more, in its measurement sentence. Nothing is FALSE; no new documentation defect is created, but one is left half-open.**

1. **The scope gap (all three passages):** each says a new `nirmmita` buffer or a new `mala` **outside a block** can start with numbers an ended block left there. A `mala` declared **inside** a block is also an ordinary (persistent) allocation — the reference's own §19 says so at `:1342` ("Nor a `mala`. ... takes ordinary memory as well, which the block never reclaims") — and it can read stale bytes the same way. The passages do not say it cannot; they just do not say it can, and the advice ("write every number ... before reading it") is addressed only to the outside-a-block case. A reader with a `mala` inside a block would not know to do the same.
2. **The measurement sentence (reference only):** the `mala m[8]` measurement as stated is not reproducible from the stated setup. The program that measured it (`Claude-to-Sarvam/evidence-r176/programs/kosh_after.sm`) has a **480 MB ordinary `nirmmita` between the block and the `mala`**, and the mala reads 77 only because that allocation pushed the persistent cursor into the dead scratch. The passage omits the 480 MB. A reader who runs a block and then a `mala` directly after it would read 0, not 77.

**The Sanskrit book was changed too, and it says the same thing as the English one.** Both books gained the paragraph (+5 lines English, +4 Sanskrit). The Sanskrit is a faithful translation and the grammar holds — Claude's own note ("The Sanskrit paragraph is mine and has not been read by anyone who would catch an error in it", R179 WS3) is answered: it was read, and there is no error in it. The one correction the Sanskrit needs is the same scope correction the English needs.

### 3.0 The three changed passages, quoted exactly (v319-book branch `12ef6329`)

The v319-book diff (`4c1159be..12ef6329`) is 7 files, 24 insertions: `Sutram-Setup.exe` (binary, 2066432 → 2067456 bytes), `Sutram-Setup.sha256` (1 line changed), `docs/CHANGELOG.md` (+3), `docs/INSTALLER-REBUILD.md` (+3), `docs/LANGUAGE-REFERENCE.md` (+8), `docs/sutram-book-english.html` (+5), `docs/sutram-book-sanskrit.html` (+4). The three passages the brief means are the reference §19 bullet and the two book paragraphs. The other two hunks (CHANGELOG, INSTALLER-REBUILD) are records of the change, not passages about the block's memory; they are checked in §3.4.

**Passage 1 — the reference, §19, `docs/LANGUAGE-REFERENCE.md:1354-1361`:**

> - **What a block gives back is not cleared (a known defect, v315-v319; found R172).** A later *block* sees zeros because its own `nirmmita` clears what it takes. An ordinary `nirmmita` or a `mala` outside any block clears nothing: it counts on memory that was never used. So once a program's blocks and its ordinary allocations have, between them, used more than the 1 GiB field, a new `nirmmita` buffer or a new `mala` outside a block can start with numbers an ended block left there, not with 0. Measured: after a block that filled 600 MB, a new `nirmmita(600000000)` read the block's value in 15,782,272 of its 75,000,000 words, and a new `mala m[8]` read 77 in `m[0]` and `m[7]`. Below 1 GiB in all it does not happen. **Until it is fixed: in a program that uses that much memory, write every number of a new buffer or `mala` outside a block before reading it.**

**Passage 2 — the English book, `docs/sutram-book-english.html:793-797`:**

> <p><b>One exception to "zeroed" (up to compiler v319):</b> what a block gives back is not cleared. Once a program's blocks and its allocations outside blocks have together used more than the 1 GiB field, a new <code>nirmmita</code> buffer or a new <code>mala</code> outside a block can begin with numbers an ended block left there, not with zeros. In a program that large, write every number of a new buffer or <code>mala</code> before you read it. A smaller program never meets this.</p>

**Passage 3 — the Sanskrit book, `docs/sutram-book-sanskrit.html:719-722`:**

> <p><b>"शून्यीकृतान्" इत्यस्य एकः अपवादः (v319 पर्यन्तम्):</b> खण्डेन प्रत्यर्पिता स्मृतिः न शून्यीक्रियते। यदा क्रमादेशस्य खण्डाः खण्डात् बहिः कृतानि ग्रहणानि च मिलित्वा 1 GiB-परिमितात् क्षेत्रात् अधिकं उपयुक्तवन्तः, तदा खण्डात् बहिः नवं <code>nirmmita</code>-स्थानं नवा <code>mala</code> वा समाप्तस्य खण्डस्य पुरातनानि मूल्यानि धारयेत्, न शून्यानि। तादृशे महति क्रमादेशे पठनात् पूर्वं प्रत्येकं मूल्यं स्वयं लिखतु। लघुः क्रमादेशः एतत् कदापि न पश्यति।</p>

Literal translation: *"One exception to 'śūnyīkṛtān' [zeroed] (up to v319): the memory given back by a block is not made zero. When a program's blocks and the takings made outside a block together have used more than the 1 GiB-limited field, then outside a block a new nirmmita-place or a new mala may hold the old values of an ended block, not zeros. In such a large program, before reading, write every value yourself. A small program never sees this."*

### 3.1 Passage 1 — the reference: IMPRECISE (two points), otherwise TRUE

**What the code says (v319, `4c1159be`):**

- **(a) A block gives back, and does not clear.** `r73_gen_rewind` (`src/r65_arena.inc:404-412`) is exactly `end = the mark` — no zeroing, no unmap, no overwrite of anything. The same for `r73_gen_ret_rewind` (`:535-552`). The block's bytes stay exactly as the block left them. The passage's headline — "What a block gives back is not cleared" — is TRUE, and it is the correct correction to the R175 hand-back's conflation: v319 does not zero at exit (R165 premise correction), so "not cleared" is the true state of v319.
- **(b) A later block sees zeros because its own `nirmmita` clears what it takes.** The scratch path (`r73_gen_scratch_nirm`, `:416-460`) zeroes `ceil(size/8)*8` bytes at allocation time via the `r73_s6` qword loop (`:342-348`). TRUE for every read a program can legally make (in-bounds reads are bounded by the requested size, which is inside the zeroed span). The known caveat, shared with the existing §19 bullet at `:1352-1353` ("sees zeros, never the earlier block's data") and established in R163 Task 3: the 8-byte alignment tail `[ceil(size/8)*8, align16(size))` is un-zeroed when `size mod 16` is 1..8, reachable only through unchecked access. "Clears what it takes" is exact for the requested size and loose for the full aligned block — but the passage repeats the existing bullet's wording rather than creating a new claim, so this is a shared imprecision, not a new defect. (Note: the header comment at `:423` says `rep stosb`; the emitted template is the qword loop — the comment is stale, the template is what runs; R165 brief correction 5.)
- **(c) An ordinary `nirmmita` or a `mala` outside any block clears nothing.** The persistent path (`r65_emit_nirm`, `src/r65_arena.inc:245`) does not zero — it loads `cur`, checks room against `end`, stores the advanced `cur`, and returns the block. The design's assumption is stated in the comment at `:23`: "Memory from the arena is never reused, so it arrives zeroed like mmap memory." TRUE. A `mala` is a persistent allocation too (`.gs_decl_array`, `:19913`, calls `r173_mala_begin` — the hoist REUSE path only — then `emit_nirmmita_from_rax`); there is no zeroing of its N elements at creation (R165 Task 3, §3.2). TRUE.
- **(d) The 1 GiB condition.** "Once a program's blocks and its ordinary allocations have, between them, used more than the 1 GiB field." The overlap happens exactly when the persistent cursor `cur` (growing up from the base) passes the lowest address the scratch cursor `end` ever reached (growing down from `sima` = base + 1 GiB) — i.e. when persistent bytes + scratch bytes exceed 1 GiB. The passage states the sum condition, which is the meeting condition. TRUE. And "Below 1 GiB in all it does not happen" is TRUE for the same reason: below 1 GiB total, `cur` never enters dead scratch.
- **(e) The hoist is the loop's reuse, not the block's giving-back.** A hoisted `kosh` resets only `[d-16]` (length) on reuse (`r172_decl_begin`, `:11051`); a hoisted `mala` zeroes its N elements on reuse (`r173_mala_begin`, `:11096`, `rep stosq` over exactly N qwords). Neither is the block clearing anything — the block still gives back without clearing. The passage does not claim otherwise. TRUE by omission (it does not confuse the two).

**The measurements — verified against the evidence files:**

- **"a new `nirmmita(600000000)` read the block's value in 15,782,272 of its 75,000,000 words" — TRUE.** `Claude-to-Sarvam/evidence-r175/programs/st_big.sm`: a block writes 77 into every word of `nirmmita(600000000)` (75,000,000 words), then a new `nirmmita(600000000)` counts the words that read 77. `evidence-r175/results-stale.txt` `[v318]`: "reads 77 in 15782272 of 75000000 words; the first at word 59217728". The number is exact. (The `[v319]` line in the same file reads 0 — that is the step-2 build *with* the exit zeroing, which the R175 hand-back conflated with the staged v319; the staged v319 `4c1159be` has no zeroing, so it reads 15,782,272 like v318. The passage's "v315-v319" is correct for the staged v319.) The geometry checks: the overlap is `[sima - 600,000,000, base + 600,000,000)` = 600,000,000 − (1,073,741,824 − 600,000,000) = 126,258,176 bytes = 15,782,272 words.
- **"a new `mala m[8]` read 77 in `m[0]` and `m[7]`" — TRUE as to what was printed, IMPRECISE as to the setup.** `Claude-to-Sarvam/evidence-r176/programs/kosh_after.sm`:
  ```sm
  mukhya() {
      mandapa {
          vitti p = nirmmita(600000000)
          chakra i = 0 tak 15782271 {
              p[i] = 77
          }
      }
      vitti q = nirmmita(480000000)
      q[0] = 1
      mala m[8]
      likha("a mala m[8] declared after 480 MB of ordinary memory and the block: m[0] ", m[0], " m[7] ", m[7], "\n")
  }
  ```
  The program prints `m[0]` and `m[7]`, and both read 77 — the geometry checks (the mala's elements land at `[base+480,000,008, base+480,000,072)`, entirely inside the written region `[base+473,741,824, base+600,000,000)`; all eight elements would read 77, the program prints two of them). But the program has a **480 MB ordinary `nirmmita` between the block and the `mala`**, and the mala reads 77 only because that allocation pushed `cur` into the dead scratch. Without it, the mala would sit at `[base+8, base+72)` — virgin memory — and read 0. The passage's sentence ("after a block that filled 600 MB, ... a new `mala m[8]` read 77") omits the 480 MB. A reader who runs a block and then a `mala` directly after it would NOT see 77. The passage's own general condition states that ordinary allocations are part of the trigger, so a careful reader can infer it — but a measurement sentence should state its setup, and this one does not. (Two smaller notes on the same sentence: "a block that filled 600 MB" is loose — the block *allocated* 600 MB of scratch; in `st_big.sm` it wrote 77 into every word, in `kosh_after.sm` into only the first 15,782,272 words (126 MB). And the program's own label says "after 480 MB of ordinary memory and the block" — the passage drops the "480 MB of ordinary memory" half of its own source's label.)
- **"(a known defect, v315-v319; found R172)" — TRUE, with a note on the range.** "Found R172" is correct: the R172 hand-back (`Claude-to-Sarvam/R172-00-A-KOSH-DECLARED-IN-A-CHAKRA-IS-MADE-ONCE-NOT-REWOUND-NOTHING-ZEROED-AND-THE-HIGH-WATER-HAZARD-IS-ALREADY-LIVE-IN-V315.md:167`) says "The stale-memory defect (WS1.3) - found, measured, named in the release notes on the branch; not fixed", with the original measurement (a 900 MB block, a 400 MB ordinary `nirmmita` reading 283 words that are not 0 — the same shape as `kosh_after.sm`, and also with an ordinary allocation between the block and the read). The R173 hand-back confirms "Measured first (`results-stale.txt`, v315 and v318 alike)". So "v315-v319" is the measured range plus v319 (same exit code as v315). Note only: the underlying geometry (the 1 GiB arena, two cursors) dates to v263 — commit `8e6ae350`, "R65 (2/2): Step 1 - nirmmita is a contiguous bump, kosh grows in place (v263)", which introduced `R65_ARENA` (0x40000000) — so the defect is almost certainly older than v315. The passage does not claim v315 is where it began, so this is a note, not a correction: the range is the range where it was measured, not necessarily the full affected range.

**The scope gap — the one imprecision that matters.** The passage says "a new `nirmmita` buffer or a new `mala` **outside a block** can start with numbers an ended block left there" and advises "write every number of a new buffer or `mala` **outside a block** before reading it". But a `mala` declared **inside** a block is also an ordinary (persistent) allocation: `.gs_decl_array` calls `emit_nirmmita_from_rax` unconditionally — there is no scratch rewrite for a `mala` declaration, no `BN_MALA_SCRATCH` exists — so a `mala` inside a block is allocated from `cur` exactly like one outside it, and if `cur` has walked into dead scratch it reads stale bytes the same way. The R179 hand-back says so plainly: "Inside a block a `mala` takes ordinary memory too, so the same could happen there, but it is not what was measured." The reference's own §19 says so too, at `:1342` ("Nor a `mala`. ... takes ordinary memory as well, which the block never reclaims") — see §3.3. So the passage's "outside any block" is narrower than the defect. It does not say a `mala` inside a block is safe — it just does not mention it — and the advice, addressed only to the outside-a-block case, would leave a reader with a `mala` inside a block unwarned. (The hoist does not close this: a hoisted `mala` is zeroed on REUSE, but its first pass is a fresh persistent allocation with no creation zeroing, so even a hoisted `mala` can read stale on pass 1 if it lands on dead scratch. An ordinary `nirmmita` written inside a block IS scratch and IS zeroed — the gap is the `mala` only, plus a `nirmmita` written inside a `prakriya` called from a block, which stays ordinary by the lexical rewrite; the passage's "outside a block" covers that one lexically, since it is not written in a block's lines.)

**Verdict on Passage 1: IMPRECISE.** True as far as it goes; two corrections needed (the scope, §3.6; the measurement setup, §3.6).

### 3.2 Passage 2 — the English book: IMPRECISE (the same scope gap), otherwise TRUE

The English book paragraph sits immediately after the paragraph that says `nirmmita(n)` gives "fresh, zeroed memory" (`:788-792`), which is the sentence it qualifies ("One exception to 'zeroed'"). Check by claim:

- "what a block gives back is not cleared" — TRUE (§3.1(a)).
- "Once a program's blocks and its allocations outside blocks have together used more than the 1 GiB field" — TRUE (§3.1(d); same sum condition as the reference).
- "a new `nirmmita` buffer or a new `mala` outside a block can begin with numbers an ended block left there, not with zeros" — TRUE for the cases it names, IMPRECISE in scope: a `mala` inside a block can too (§3.1).
- "In a program that large, write every number of a new buffer or `mala` before you read it." — TRUE as advice, and note the English book's advice does NOT repeat "outside a block" (unlike the reference's) — it says "a new buffer or `mala`", which in context means the ones the preceding sentence named. The omission is harmless here, but it does not fix the scope gap either: a reader would still read the preceding sentence as scoping the advice to the outside-a-block case.
- "A smaller program never meets this." — TRUE ("Below 1 GiB in all it does not happen", §3.1(d)).
- "(up to compiler v319)" — TRUE and consistent with the reference's "v315-v319" (the defect is present through v319; the exit zeroing, if it ships as v320, would end it).

**Verdict on Passage 2: IMPRECISE** (the scope gap only). The English book does not give measurements, so it does not carry the reference's measurement imprecision.

### 3.3 Passage 3 — the Sanskrit book: IMPRECISE (the same scope gap), otherwise TRUE — and it says the same thing as the English one

**Both books were changed.** The diff touches both (`+5` English, `+4` Sanskrit), in the same place (after the paragraph that says `nirmmita(n)` gives fresh, zeroed memory). The Sanskrit book does not still carry the OLD sentence — the old sentence was the unqualified "fresh, zeroed memory", and both books now carry the exception paragraph after it.

**Word-by-word comparison with the English (literal translation in §3.0):**

| English | Sanskrit | Same? |
|---|---|---|
| "One exception to 'zeroed' (up to compiler v319)" | `"शून्यीकृतान्" इत्यस्य एकः अपवादः (v319 पर्यन्तम्)` — "One exception to 'śūnyīkṛtān' (up to v319)" | Yes. The Sanskrit quotes the exact word the preceding paragraph uses (`नवान् शून्यीकृतान् ... स्मृतिभागान् ददाति`, `:715` — "gives new, zeroed, n-byte-sized memory-parts"), just as the English quotes "zeroed". |
| "what a block gives back is not cleared" | `खण्डेन प्रत्यर्पिता स्मृतिः न शून्यीक्रियते` — "the memory given back by a block is not made zero" | Yes. |
| "Once a program's blocks and its allocations outside blocks have together used more than the 1 GiB field" | `यदा क्रमादेशस्य खण्डाः खण्डात् बहिः कृतानि ग्रहणानि च मिलित्वा 1 GiB-परिमितात् क्षेत्रात् अधिकं उपयुक्तवन्तः` — "When a program's blocks and the takings made outside a block together have used more than the 1 GiB-limited field" | Yes. (`ग्रहणानि` = "takings, allocations" — the book's word for an allocation.) |
| "a new `nirmmita` buffer or a new `mala` outside a block can begin with numbers an ended block left there, not with zeros" | `तदा खण्डात् बहिः नवं nirmmita-स्थानं नवा mala वा समाप्तस्य खण्डस्य पुरातनानि मूल्यानि धारयेत्, न शून्यानि` — "then outside a block a new nirmmita-place or a new mala may hold the old values of an ended block, not zeros" | Yes. (`nirmmita-स्थानम्` = "a nirmmita-place", the book's rendering of a buffer; `धारयेत्` = "may hold", optative, matching "can begin with".) |
| "In a program that large, write every number of a new buffer or `mala` before you read it." | `तादृशे महति क्रमादेशे पठनात् पूर्वं प्रत्येकं मूल्यं स्वयं लिखतु` — "In such a large program, before reading, write every value yourself." | Yes, in context. The Sanskrit says "every value" where the English says "every number of a new buffer or `mala`" — the preceding sentence supplies the object. (Note: `लिखतु` is the 3rd-person imperative, the standard Sanskrit form for a general instruction — "one should write" — where the English uses the 2nd person. A style choice, not an error.) |
| "A smaller program never meets this." | `लघुः क्रमादेशः एतत् कदापि न पश्यति` — "A small program never sees this." | Yes. |

**Grammar check (Claude's note in R179 WS3: "The Sanskrit paragraph is mine and has not been read by anyone who would catch an error in it"):** it was read, and there is no error in it. `खण्डेन प्रत्यर्पिता स्मृतिः न शून्यीक्रियते` — instrumental agent, nominative feminine subject agreeing with its participle, passive present 3rd singular — correct. `उपयुक्तवन्तः` (masculine plural perfect participle) agrees with `खण्डाः` (the first of the two subjects joined by `च`) — acceptable Sanskrit for a mixed subject. `धारयेत्` (optative 3rd singular) agrees with either singular subject (`नवं ...-स्थानम्`, neuter, or `नवा mala`, feminine). `शून्यीकृतान्` is the correct accusative plural of `शून्यीकृत` and matches the preceding paragraph's word exactly. No wrong case, no wrong agreement, no wrong word.

**The one correction the Sanskrit needs is the same scope correction the English needs** (§3.6): `खण्डात् बहिः` ("outside a block") qualifies the `mala` as well as the `nirmmita`-place, and a `mala` inside a block is missing from it, exactly as in the English.

**Verdict on Passage 3: IMPRECISE** (the scope gap only). The two books say the same thing; neither was left unchanged.

### 3.4 The reference (§19): does the new passage agree with the rest of §19, and with MEMORY-MODEL.md?

**With `:1322` ("A `kosh` of *numbers* used in a block works; its storage is ordinary memory, which the block does not reclaim") — agrees.** That sentence is about a `kosh` in a block (ordinary memory, not reclaimed). The new passage is about ordinary `nirmmita`/`mala` allocations. No contradiction. (R162/R164 said `:1322` remains TRUE under the hoist; the new passage does not touch it, and nothing in it weakens it. Note the line numbers have shifted since R164 cited `:1291-1292` — on the v319-book branch the sentence is at `:1322-1323`, the "Nor a `mala`." paragraph at `:1342-1345`, and "sees zeros" at `:1352-1353`.)

**With `:1342-1345` ("Nor a `mala`. ... takes ordinary memory as well, which the block never reclaims") — agrees, and the agreement is what makes the scope gap visible.** `:1342` already says a `mala` inside a block is ordinary memory. The new passage's "outside any block" is therefore narrower than the reference's own model: the reference knows a `mala` inside a block is ordinary, but the new passage warns only about one outside a block. The new passage does not contradict `:1342` — it just does not cover the case `:1342` implies. The correction (§3.6) brings the two into line.

**With `:1352-1353` ("A later block reuses the same memory, and sees zeros, never the earlier block's data") — agrees.** The new passage's "A later *block* sees zeros because its own `nirmmita` clears what it takes" is the same claim, with the reason added. Both share the alignment-tail caveat (§3.1(b)) — the new passage does not create it, it repeats it.

**With MEMORY-MODEL.md — agrees, and the new passage goes one step further.** The status box's line 5 (mandapa scope) says "A `mandapa` block gives back, when it ends, what `nirmmita` written in its own lines took - and nothing else" — the new passage ("what a block gives back is not cleared") is consistent with it: giving back is not clearing. The "Known defect, not fixed in v319" bullet says "an ordinary `nirmmita` taken after a block ended can be handed memory the block wrote, once the block's side and the ordinary side of the 1 GiB field have used the same addresses (measured: 15,782,272 words of a 600 MB block's data in a 600 MB buffer)" — the same measurement, the same condition, and the new passage agrees with it. (That bullet is `docs/MEMORY-MODEL.md:9` on the v319-book branch `12ef6329` — it arrived with the v319 staging, not with the v319-book diff, and it is not on main at `441a9baa`.) The new passage goes further than MEMORY-MODEL.md in one respect: it names the `mala` (MEMORY-MODEL.md's defect bullet names only the ordinary `nirmmita`). That is an addition, not a contradiction — and it is the addition that makes the scope gap matter, since MEMORY-MODEL.md's bullet does not warn about a `mala` at all. (The status box's line 5 also notes, as a measured R166 fact, that a `mala` made inside a block keeps its bytes for the whole program — which is the same ordinary-memory fact the scope correction in §3.6 rests on.) The `parimana()` line (`:12`) is untouched by the new passage and unaffected by it: the defect does not change what `parimana()` counts (it counts capacity/logical bytes held, and a stale-filled buffer counts the same as a zero-filled one).

### 3.5 The installer: "Compiler bytes untouched, so the guard is identity" — TRUE, with the caveat that the guard never looks at the installer

**"Compiler bytes untouched" — TRUE.** The v319-book diff touches no compiler source and no compiler binary: `src/` is unchanged, `win/sutram.exe` is not in the diff (the diff is `Sutram-Setup.exe`, `Sutram-Setup.sha256`, and five docs files only). The CHANGELOG entry says "Documents only; the compiler's bytes are those of 4c1159be", and the INSTALLER-REBUILD.md entry says "the compiler and every other binary are unchanged". The installer grew by 1,024 bytes (2,066,432 → 2,067,456) — consistent with embedding the new doc text (the reference +8 lines, the books +5/+4, CHANGELOG +3, INSTALLER-REBUILD +3). `Sutram-Setup.sha256` changed by one line (the hash `76cf5153...` → `e84c39a2...`). The installer's contents cannot be verified by reading (it is a binary), so the claim that only the docs changed inside it rests on the rebuild record — but nothing in the tree contradicts it, and the tree's own compiler binary is byte-unchanged.

**"So the guard is identity" — TRUE, but the guard is not the evidence for the installer.** The guard (`tools/guard_pair.py`) compiles every program in `examples/*.sm`, `examples/*/*.sm`, `tests/stdlib/*.sm`, `tests/r*/*.sm`, `tests/r*/*/*.sm` with two (tree, compiler) pairs and compares the output bytes. It never looks at the installer, the docs, or `Sutram-Setup.exe`. With the compiler byte-identical and the `.sm` sources unchanged, every program compiles to the same bytes — 0 DIFFER — so the guard is identity. But that identity says nothing about the installer: the guard runs the tree's compiler, not the installer's payload, so even if the installer rebuild had changed the embedded compiler, the guard would not see it. The evidence that the installer's compiler is unchanged is the rebuild record (INSTALLER-REBUILD.md) plus the fact that `win/sutram.exe` is not in the diff — not the guard. The installer rebuild is unrelated to the three passages except that it packages the new docs; it is not a change the guard would see, and it does not break the "guard is identity" claim, because the guard's claim is about compiled program bytes, not about the installer.

### 3.6 Paste-ready corrections

**The scope correction — for all three passages.** The defect is not "outside a block"; it is "any ordinary allocation", and a `mala` is an ordinary allocation wherever it is declared. The correction is the same in all three passages: widen the `mala` (and only the `mala` — an ordinary `nirmmita` written inside a block IS scratch and IS zeroed).

**Reference (§19, `:1354-1361`), corrected sentences:**

> - **What a block gives back is not cleared (a known defect, v315-v319; found R172).** A later *block* sees zeros because its own `nirmmita` clears what it takes. An ordinary `nirmmita` outside any block clears nothing: it counts on memory that was never used. A `mala` clears nothing wherever it is declared — inside a block it takes ordinary memory too. So once a program's blocks and its ordinary allocations have, between them, used more than the 1 GiB field, a new `nirmmita` buffer outside a block, or a new `mala` anywhere, can start with numbers an ended block left there, not with 0. Measured: [as below]. Below 1 GiB in all it does not happen. **Until it is fixed: in a program that uses that much memory, write every number of a new buffer outside a block, or of a new `mala` anywhere, before reading it.**

**English book (`:793-797`), corrected:**

> <p><b>One exception to "zeroed" (up to compiler v319):</b> what a block gives back is not cleared. Once a program's blocks and its allocations outside blocks have together used more than the 1 GiB field, a new <code>nirmmita</code> buffer outside a block, or a new <code>mala</code> anywhere, can begin with numbers an ended block left there, not with zeros. In a program that large, write every number of a new buffer or <code>mala</code> before you read it. A smaller program never meets this.</p>

**Sanskrit book (`:719-722`), corrected:**

> <p><b>"शून्यीकृतान्" इत्यस्य एकः अपवादः (v319 पर्यन्तम्):</b> खण्डेन प्रत्यर्पिता स्मृतिः न शून्यीक्रियते। यदा क्रमादेशस्य खण्डाः खण्डात् बहिः कृतानि ग्रहणानि च मिलित्वा 1 GiB-परिमितात् क्षेत्रात् अधिकं उपयुक्तवन्तः, तदा खण्डात् बहिः नवं <code>nirmmita</code>-स्थानं कुत्रापि नवा <code>mala</code> वा समाप्तस्य खण्डस्य पुरातनानि मूल्यानि धारयेत्, न शून्यानि। तादृशे महति क्रमादेशे पठनात् पूर्वं प्रत्येकं मूल्यं स्वयं लिखतु। लघुः क्रमादेशः एतत् कदापि न पश्यति।</p>
>
> (Literal: "...then outside a block a new nirmmita-place, or anywhere a new mala, may hold the old values of an ended block, not zeros. ...". `कुत्रापि` = "anywhere" — placed with the `mala`, so the `nirmmita`-place keeps its `खण्डात् बहिः` and the `mala` is widened to anywhere. The Sanskrit translation of the corrected sentence needs a native speaker's check before pasting, as the R164 report flagged for its Sanskrit sentence.)

**The measurement sentence — reference only (`:1358-1359`).** The 480 MB ordinary allocation between the block and the `mala` is part of the setup and must be stated. Corrected:

>   with numbers an ended block left there, not with 0. Measured: after a block that filled 600 MB, a new `nirmmita(600000000)` read the block's value in 15,782,272 of its 75,000,000 words; and after the same block and 480 MB of ordinary allocations, a new `mala m[8]` read 77 in `m[0]` and `m[7]`. Below 1 GiB in all it does not happen.

**What does NOT need changing:** "found R172" — correct; leave it. "v315-v319" — the measured range; leave it (the note in §3.1 that the geometry predates v315 is a historical observation, not a correction to the passage). The 15,782,272 / 75,000,000 figure and the `m[0]` / `m[7]` readings — exact as printed; leave them. "A later *block* sees zeros because its own `nirmmita` clears what it takes" — true for legal reads; the alignment-tail caveat is shared with the existing bullet and needs no new wording here. The CHANGELOG and INSTALLER-REBUILD.md entries — accurate records of what changed; leave them.

### 3.7 The stability question (ROUND-180 WS4): if the floor lands, does the sentence stay true?

ROUND-180 WS4: "If the floor lands, that sentence changes again. Note it; do not write it yet." The floor (Task 1, ROUND-180 WS1): the ordinary side never enters block memory — the ordinary cursor stops at a floor (the lowest address scratch ever reached), so an ordinary allocation after a block can never be handed the block's bytes. Two cursors, block memory reused by blocks and never by anything else, no zeroing at the exit.

**The headline fact stays TRUE; the passage's operative claims become FALSE.** Under the floor:

- "What a block gives back is not cleared" — **stays TRUE.** The floor does not clear the block's bytes; it makes them unreachable to ordinary allocations. The bytes are still there, with the old values, until a later block reuses the range (and a later block's `nirmmita` zeroes what it takes, as today). The sentence remains literally true.
- "A later *block* sees zeros because its own `nirmmita` clears what it takes" — **stays TRUE.** Unchanged by the floor.
- "An ordinary `nirmmita` or a `mala` outside any block clears nothing: it counts on memory that was never used" — **stays TRUE as a statement about the allocator, but its premise becomes unobservable.** The ordinary allocator still does not zero; it just can no longer be handed used memory, so the assumption it "counts on" is never violated.
- "Once a program's blocks and its ordinary allocations have, between them, used more than the 1 GiB field, a new `nirmmita` buffer or a new `mala` outside a block can start with numbers an ended block left there, not with 0" — **becomes FALSE.** Under the floor, no ordinary allocation can ever start with the block's numbers, however much memory the program uses — the ordinary cursor stops at the floor. The 1 GiB condition ceases to matter for this.
- "Below 1 GiB in all it does not happen" — **becomes vacuous.** It does not happen above 1 GiB either, under the floor.
- "**Until it is fixed: ... write every number of a new buffer or `mala` outside a block before reading it**" — **becomes unnecessary.** The advice would be harmless but pointless; a reader following it would be doing work the floor makes redundant.

**So ROUND-180 WS4 is right: the sentence changes again.** What it would need to say under the floor is the fact without the consequence — something like: "What a block gives back is not cleared, but it is never handed out again: the ordinary side stops at the floor, so no new buffer or `mala` can start with an ended block's numbers, however much memory the program uses. A later block reuses the range and sees zeros, because its own `nirmmita` clears what it takes." The "not cleared" stays; the "can start with numbers" and the "write before reading" go. (And the scope correction in §3.6 becomes moot under the floor too — a `mala` inside a block also cannot be handed the block's bytes, since its allocation is ordinary and the floor stops the ordinary side — but the sentence should still say so if it keeps the `mala` in it.)

---

## 4. Task 4 — what is left, ranked

**The ranking is a judgement, not a reading.** The evidence for each item is read from the tree and cited; the order is a view of what a student hits first and hardest. The memory work is excluded by the brief, so none of the three is memory: not the floor, not the zeroing, not `madvise`, not the hoist landing.

### 4.1 First: the lexer silently skips characters it does not know

**The thing, plainly:** a student types a character the language does not know, and the compiler deletes it and carries on. No error, no warning, no line. The program that runs is not the program the student wrote.

**The evidence.** One site, `src/sutram_compiler.asm:7420`:

```
    ; Unknown char — skip
    inc r13
    jmp .lex_loop
```

163 of the 256 byte values reach it (R160 T3, verified again at this base — the site is unchanged). Six of them are printable ASCII a student can type with no modifier key: `$` `'` `@` `\` backtick `~`. The worst case, from R160: `$total = 0` silently compiles as `total = 0` — the `$` is eaten, the assignment goes to a different name, and nothing says so. A stray backslash or a backtick does the same. A multi-byte character (an em dash, a smart quote pasted from a document) is eaten a byte at a time.

This is the one defect in the tree that no error message can help with, because the evidence is destroyed before any diagnostic exists. It also violates the project's own stated rule: the R77 stray-tokens gate (`tools/gates.sh`, "R77 stray") exists precisely so that "a stray token in every kind of body is refused or every statement runs — no silent partial compilation". The gate checks stray *tokens*; the skip happens before a token exists, so the gate cannot see it. The project has already fixed this class once — R17 D1, unary `!`, "was silently skipped", fixed by giving it a token. The lexer skip is the same class, unfixed.

**Why it matters to a student.** A beginner's most common mistake is a typo, and the most confusing kind of typo is one the tool swallows. The student reads their program, reads the output, and the two do not agree, and there is no message pointing at the cause. Every other improvement — better errors, better docs — is moot for this class, because the compiler has already thrown the character away. It is also the first thing a student hits: `$` is the habit of every shell, PHP and Perl user, and `@` is in every email address a student might paste into a comment or a string boundary.

**How big it is.** Small, and fully described — not designed here, described in R160 T3 (`~/workspace/r160/notes/r160_t3_lexer_skips.md`, §4.2), which still matches the code at this base: replace the two instructions at `:7420` with a refusal via `r80_compile_error` (`:11700`), passing `r12` (the token-start source pointer, set at `:6993`) so the student gets file, line, column and a source snippet; a new error code is needed (no unknown-character code exists — `E_UTF8` is about malformed bytes, not unrecognised characters); the message must name the character that was refused; for a multi-byte character, advance past the full UTF-8 scalar first (`r68_utf8_width`, `src/r68_unicode_helpers.inc:5`); and one carve-out is mandatory — a UTF-8 BOM (`EF BB BF`) currently reaches the skip site three times and is dropped by accident, which is the correct behaviour for a BOM, so the loader must strip one leading BOM or the lexer must skip it deliberately. Nothing else in the lexer needs to change: anything reaching that site has already failed every legitimate test (whitespace, comment, danda, digit, identifier, string, operator, delimiter), so the refusal cannot fire on valid input.

Who does it: Claude builds it (a compiler change, one site); the error code and the message wording are Sarvam's/owner's to name. It is not assigned here.

**What "done" looks like.** `$total = 0` refuses at compile time with a message naming `$`, the line, the column, and a snippet — and a hint saying what to write instead. A file with a leading BOM still compiles. Proof: a gate with goldens for each of the six printable ASCII characters, one high byte, and one BOM-marked file — the goldens being the refusal messages, so the fix is pinned the way the R77 stray gate pins stray tokens.

**Already known.** Yes — R160 T3, an open thread, fix described, not built. It makes the top three because it is the only open thread that is silent, wrong, and undetectable by the student, and because the fix is the smallest of any item on this list.

### 4.2 Second: there is no way to read text from the user

**The thing, plainly:** the language can print rich text and can only hear numbers. The most basic interactive program — "what is your name?" — cannot be written.

**The evidence.** The builtin table (`src/sutram_compiler.asm`, the `bn_table` at `:900`–`:960`) holds 48 builtins. Exactly one of them reads input: `grahan` (BN 33, `:226`), and its own comment (`:1074`) says what it does — "read one line from stdin, parse digits, return in rax". The line is read and thrown away; only the number survives. There is no string-input builtin, no library wrapper around `grahan` (zero uses of `grahan` in `lib/*.smlib`), and no example that reads text. The examples that use `grahan` (`examples/23_grahan_input.sm`, `24_guess_game.sm`, `26_calculator.sm`) all read numbers. The book mentions `grahan` only as a number reader. The reference's builtin table (`docs/LANGUAGE-REFERENCE.md:178`) says it plainly: "`grahan` | read a number from input".

Everything on the output side exists: `likha` prints text, `vartani_*` compares and joins strings, `shabda` prints a string value, `s + t` makes a new string. The input side has one function and it only speaks numbers.

**Why it matters to a student.** Every beginner course starts with input/output of text: greet by name, echo a sentence, a quiz, a text menu. A student who has just learned to print a string asks the obvious next question — how do I read one? — and the answer is that there is no way. The workaround is a raw file-descriptor read (`paadh`), which needs a hand-managed buffer and a byte count, i.e. the student must drop to syscalls to do the first thing a beginner does. A student who cannot write an interactive program in their first week concludes the language is not for writing programs, only for running them.

**How big it is.** Medium — a new builtin, and its name is a vocabulary decision (Sarvam's/owner's; the root-versus-noun rule from R108/R109 applies). The shape is visible in the tree: the string representation is a bare NUL-terminated pointer (or a pointer/length pair in the `khand` world), and the `s + t` allocation pattern shows how a new string is made (`nirmmita` + copy). The builtin needs to read a line, put it in a buffer the student can keep, and return it as a string value — the same shape as `grahan`, with the line kept instead of parsed. Docs and a golden go with it, in the same commit, per the repo's rule (AGENTS.md: "Docs are part of the change"). Who builds it: Claude (compiler change); who names it: Sarvam/owner. Not assigned here.

**What "done" looks like.** A program that reads a name and prints `Namaste, <name>` compiles and prints the name back. Proof: that program as a golden in the suite, next to `examples/23_grahan_input.sm`, and a reference row next to `grahan`'s saying what it returns and how long the result lives.

**Already known.** No — as far as this reading can tell this is not in the open threads. It was checked against the list the brief gave (lexer skip, `r108` statement-call bypass, mala-escape refusal, un-totalled lists, the nineteen doc claims, the build-integrity manifest, the lock, self-overwrite, the dead parameter-growth message, `patra_asti`, the unchecked-pointer segfaults, scientific notation, tiny dasham, silent dasham overflow, the REPL banner, the 16 library contracts, R119 cleanup, R120 goldens). None of them names text input. It is a gap, not a defect, which is presumably why no round has raised it: nothing is broken, something is missing. That is also why it ranks second — a missing capability the student meets in week one, not a bug they meet by accident.

### 4.3 Third: reading a file is the one basic thing the language can do that no example shows and the reference mislabels

**The thing, plainly:** the library can read a file, but no example in the tree reads one, and the reference's builtin table swaps the two raw builtins a student would need. The student who writes a file in one lesson and tries to read it in the next has nothing to copy and a table that points them at the wrong builtin.

**The evidence.** The capability exists and is correct: `patra_path_sarv(path, out, cap)` (`lib/patra.smlib:29`–`:56`) opens a path read-only, loops `paadh` until the count is met or EOF, closes the descriptor, and returns the byte count read (or -1 on any error). The module header (`lib/patra.smlib:1`–`:27`) documents the contract honestly, including that the descriptor is always closed before returning.

But nothing teaches it. Zero of the 331 top-level examples reads a file: no example uses `paadh`, `dvaram(`, or `patra_` at all (grep over `examples/*.sm`). The book's file chapter, if it has one, has no reading example to point at. And the reference's builtin table (`docs/LANGUAGE-REFERENCE.md:180`–`:181`) has the two raw builtins backwards:

| `dvaram` | patra descriptor helper |
| `paadh` | open a file |

The code says the opposite: `dvaram(path, flags[, mode])` is the open (`src/sutram_compiler.asm:22702`, "R52: dvaram(path, flags[, mode]) -> Linux sys_open result"), and `paadh(fd, buf, count)` is the read (`:22751`, "paadh(fd, buf, count) -> bytes_read (sys_read=0)"). A student who follows the table to open a file calls the read builtin with a path, and gets a refusal or a wrong answer with no indication the table sent them to the wrong row.

Two further facts belong to this item. First, the read function needs a pre-allocated buffer and a cap the student must guess (`out`, `cap`) — there is no "read the whole file" shape, so a student who does not know the file's size in advance must guess a cap and check the count. Second, the third function in the module, `patra_asti(path)` (`lib/patra.smlib:81`–`:90`), is wrong: it answers "does this file exist" by opening it read-only, which says yes for a directory and cannot distinguish missing from unreadable, and it hangs on a FIFO (R116). The correct check needs a stat-like builtin, which was specified and STOPPED (R189) — so that part of this item is not a small fix.

**Why it matters to a student.** Writing a file and reading it back is the second half of the first file lesson, and it is the first thing a student does with data that outlives the program. The gap is not in the compiler (the function works) but in the path to it: no example to copy, a table that misnames the two builtins, and a read function whose buffer-and-cap shape is never demonstrated. A student who hits this wastes their time in exactly the place where the language should be most helpful — the standard library doing the obvious thing.

**How big it is.** Small for the teaching half: one example (write a file, read it back, print it) and two corrected table rows — a documentation and example change, not a compiler change, and the repo's rule says docs go in the same commit as the behaviour they describe (here the behaviour already exists, so the docs are the change). Bigger for `patra_asti`: the fix needs the stat builtin (R189, stopped), which is Sarvam's/owner's decision. Who does the small half: the owner/Claude. Not assigned here.

**What "done" looks like.** An example that writes a file with `patra_likh_sarv`, reads it back with `patra_path_sarv`, and prints what it read — with a golden, so the read path is pinned the way the write path already is. The two table rows corrected (`dvaram` opens, `paadh` reads). Proof: the example's golden, and the table rows matching the code comments at `:22702` and `:22751`.

**Already known.** Partly. `patra_asti` is a known stopped contract (R112/R189) — that half is not new. The two new findings from this reading are the swapped table rows and the zero-examples fact: counted at this base (no example in `examples/*.sm` uses `paadh`, `dvaram(`, or `patra_`), and the rows at `:180`–`:181` say the opposite of the code. This ranks third because it is the smallest of the three — a teaching gap, not a compiler gap — and because the capability itself already exists.

### 4.4 What was deliberately left out

**The memory work** — the floor, the zeroing, `madvise`, the hoist landing, the 38 DIFFER question. Excluded by the brief, and in any case not what a student hits first: a student does not meet the arena, they meet the lexer, the input, and the file.

**The 29-locals segfault (R29, not fixed, no gate).** A function with 29+ locals across nested blocks segfaults with no message (28 fine, 29 crashes in chakravala). Severe, but rare — a beginner's functions are small — and it is a compiler-correctness bug in the audit queue, not a student-facing gap. First below the line; if the ranking were by severity alone it would be first.

**"Did you mean" for a typo'd name.** `E_UNDEFINED_NAME` already gives the line, the name, and a hint ("declare before use", plus the old-keyword suggestion at `src/sutram_compiler.asm:332`–`:337`). A suggestion would help, but the student usually spots their own typo from the line; modest marginal gain for medium work in NASM.

**The numeric display.** `likha` prints six decimals (`src/sutram_compiler.asm:1141`, "T12 fixed-six-decimal float printer"), so a nonzero value below half a millionth prints `0.000000`, and `1e5` is refused on input while the printer itself emits `d.dddddde+N` for large values (`examples/296_r93_float_e_notation.sm`). But both behaviours are documented in the reference (§15, `:835`, `:900`–`:904` — "A tiny number prints as `0.000000` but is not zero"), and the library has the workaround (`roop_sthir_mool`, `lib/roop.smlib:474`, places up to 9). Documented quirks with a workaround, not traps.

**The docs' wrong numbers.** The README says 222 examples and 305 functions (`README.md:55`, `:58`); the tree has 331 examples and 310 functions (R159 P5/P6). The nineteen wrong claims are already being fixed by Claude (R154 T1). A fix pass, already owned; the README numbers are a one-line fix.

**The installer build.** `windows/windows-build-integrity.sha256` is 200/440 at this base — the installer build cannot proceed until it is deliberately regenerated after review (R159). Release process, already owned by Claude/owner; a student gets the last built installer meanwhile. Not a language improvement.

**`vartani_tul` returns 0 when equal.** `==` on two strings works (v287, compares contents), so a student rarely needs the raw comparator, and the sort example documents the `> 0` use (`docs/LANGUAGE-REFERENCE.md:1129`). A doc row, not a top-three item.

**The REPL lists `nirgat` while `tyaj` is primary.** Both are accepted as exit commands (`src/sutram_compiler.asm:2306`, `:2311`, `:2354`), so it is cosmetic. The packs are frozen anyway (LANGUAGE-PRIORITY.md).

**The statement-call `r108` bypass (R162/R164) and the mala-escape refusal question (R164 T2).** Compiler-correctness and language-design items, not things a student hits while learning; the hoist's scan already protects the student from the consequence.

**The un-totalled-list class (R159), the lock, the self-overwrite fix, the dead parameter-growth message, the R119 cleanup candidates, the R120 no-provenance goldens, the unchecked-pointer segfault class (`sankhya_yog`/`sankhya_gunan`).** Internal hygiene and correctness items; a student does not meet them in normal use. (`sankhya_yog`/`sankhya_gunan` are raw SIMD builtins; a beginner never calls them.)

**The 128 KiB expanded-source cap** (`IMPORT_BUF_CAP`, `src/sutram_compiler.asm:33`) and the ~10-module import limit. A student importing a handful of modules is fine, and the refusal exists ("vistarit srot seema atikram", `:1437`).

**Error messages in Sanskrit.** By design (Sanskrit-only until v1.0); the E-codes are English and the reference documents them; the packs cover ten other Indian languages. A friction for an English-only student, but a deliberate design, not a defect.

**One correction to the open-threads list, found while reading.** `likha(kosh)` printing an address (R100 trap 5) is **fixed**, not still live. `.ge_call` calls `r126_check_builtin` for every builtin call (`src/sutram_compiler.asm:22055`), and `r126_check_builtin` (`:29278`) refuses a kosh as any argument except arg0 of a kosh builtin — so `likha(k)` is refused with `E_KOSH_DVARA` and a message naming the use (`r126_d_raw`, `:619`). The "still live" note was true at v289/v290, before the R126 doors at v301; it is stale at this base. The remaining address-printing case is a raw pointer, which is a number in this language by design. (Parent-verified: `.ge_call` calls `r126_check_builtin` first, before the `BN_LIKHA` dispatch at `:22073` and the `.ge_call_likha` handler at `:22788`; the door message at `:619` names likha explicitly: "' smriti-sthanam iva na upayujyate (likha, pad8, likh8 ...)".)

---

## 5. Brief corrections

1. **v320 IS on origin.** The shared state says "v320 `9bb059ea` — the block-exit zeroing. 38 DIFFER, exactly the programs with a block. Not ready." It does not say its code is unreadable, but the round's working premise (and the T1 note's could-not-determine) took it that way. It is branch `r176-block-exit-zero` at `9bb059ea`, and this round read it first-hand (the pin change in §2.4 was verified on the branch).
2. **"The floor … changes the same 38 programs" and "the 38 is the minimum" are implementation-dependent.** True only under implementation B (split `end`). Under implementation A (a new global `L`) the byte-DIFFER is 404, not 38 (§1.6). The placement table does not name an implementation, so the claim is unfounded as written.
3. **"The project's rule is 0 DIFFER: no program's bytes may change" is a shorthand that drops the qualification.** The rule as written is 0 DIFFER for programs that do not use a `mandapa`, and for the rest a stated list with every changed program producing the same output (§2.1–§2.2).
4. **ROUND-180 WS3's framing ("It asserted 99 and now gets 0 — it was checking the bug") is wrong about what the program pins.** `blind_kosh_via_call.sm` pins that the program compiles and reads through a kept scratch address — a hole in the escape check — not the value 99 (§2.4). Claude makes the same correction in the R179 hand-back §3.
5. **The R179 hand-back's conflation of the staged v319 with the step-2 build.** The `results-stale.txt` `[v319]` line reads 0 — that is the step-2 build *with* the exit zeroing, not the staged v319 `4c1159be`, which has no zeroing and reads 15,782,272 like v318 (§3.1). The reference passage's "v315-v319" is correct for the staged v319.
6. **"a block that filled 600 MB" is loose.** The block *allocated* 600 MB of scratch; in `st_big.sm` it wrote 77 into every word, in `kosh_after.sm` into only the first 15,782,272 words (126 MB) (§3.1).
7. **The `mala m[8]` measurement as stated is not reproducible.** The program has a 480 MB ordinary `nirmmita` between the block and the `mala`, and the mala reads 77 only because that allocation pushed `cur` into the dead scratch (§3.1). The passage omits the 480 MB.
8. **The reference's `dvaram`/`paadh` rows are swapped.** The table says `dvaram` = "patra descriptor helper" and `paadh` = "open a file"; the code says `dvaram` opens (`:22702`) and `paadh` reads (`:22751`) (§4.3).
9. **R100 trap 5 (`likha(kosh)` prints an address) is fixed, not still live.** The R126 doors refuse `likha(k)` at compile time (v301); the "still live" note was true at v289/v290 (§4.4).
10. **ROUND-180 WS1's framing ("If a block's allocations and ordinary allocations share one cursor…") is wrong on the facts.** They do not share one cursor — there are already two (`cur` up, `end` down) — and the bug is that the persistent side's limit (`end`) is restored upward at block exit (§1.1).
11. **Gate counts — the "85 PASS" status line is off by one, one gate higher.** v319's `tools/gates.sh` has 82 `run_gate` invocations (v318: 81, main: 78), so the total is 85 entries but the maximum PASS is **84**, not 85. The branch's "85 PASS" status line and commit message carry the R159 P4 off-by-one, one gate higher. On main the reference still says "81 PASS" where max PASS is 80. (Also: the R179 hand-back's "gates: 85 PASS / 0 FAIL / 1 NOT-RUN" for the documents branch carries the same off-by-one.)

