## 6. Could not determine (from source — read-only round, nothing run)

- Whether the `mala m[8]` in `kosh_after.sm` actually prints 77 for `m[0]` and `m[7]` when run, and whether `m[1]`–`m[6]` also read 77 (the geometry says they would, but only two were printed) — not run, by rule. The traces are from the emitted code and the evidence files' own labels, not from execution.
- Whether the installer's embedded compiler is byte-identical to `win/sutram.exe` in the tree — the installer is a binary and cannot be read here; the claim rests on the rebuild record (`docs/INSTALLER-REBUILD.md`) and on `win/sutram.exe` not being in the diff. (The guard would not catch a difference either way, §3.5.)
- Whether the defect exists before v315 — the geometry (1 GiB arena, two cursors) dates to v263, so it almost certainly does, but it was only measured on v315/v316/v317/v318; no pre-v315 measurement exists in the tree.
- The exact runtime behaviour of a `mala` inside a block reading stale bytes (the scope-gap case) — not run, by rule; the trace is from the allocation paths (`.gs_decl_array` → `emit_nirmmita_from_rax`, no creation zeroing), not from execution.
- Whether the v320 guard run (549 identical / 196 refused / 38 DIFFER) was produced by the exact command in `tools/guard_pair.py`'s docstring, and whether the four runs (ELF/PE × v318/v315) were run from the same built binaries — the evidence files state the counts, not the commands; nothing was run here.
- Whether gate `R73 scratch` passes on the v320 branch with 35 checks — the R179 hand-back states it; not verified by running here.
- Whether the 84 DIFFER in evidence-r129 were all slice programs by the same checkable rule as the 38 (the file states the count and that all 84 had the same output; the set's defining rule is not stated in the file itself).
- Whether the owner would accept Claude's proposed amendment text ("0 DIFFER outside the declared set; the declared set equals the differing set; every program in it run both ways") — that is his decision, not a reading.
- Whether Sarvam, as the round-assigner, would accept the 38 landing under the rule as written rather than under an amended rule — that is the landing decision (§2.5), not a reading.
- Whether the floor lands, and in which form — the stability answer in §3.7 assumes the floor as described (two cursors, block memory never reused by the ordinary path, no zeroing at the exit).
- Whether the `r38_nirm_mmap` fallback is counted in `parimana()`. The R107 comment sums three terms (arena persistent, arena scratch, kosh regions); the fallback mmap is none of them, so a persistent allocation that falls back after a block is presumably invisible to `parimana()` — which would make `parimana()` **under**-report under implementation A in the nearly-full case. Not verified from the fallback path.
- Whether any gate outside `tests/expect/` asserts the `317`/`318` values. No `.py` reference was found by grep; the gate runner may reference them by number, which was not traced.
- Exact per-allocation instruction counts for the low-water update. No floor implementation exists to measure; the "~3–4 instructions per scratch allocation" is from the shape of the code (load, compare, conditional store), not a build.
- Windows (PE). The floor is arena-cursor logic in the platform-shared emitters, but the PE path was not verified separately, and the R179 hand-back notes the Windows runtime answers an unknown system call with -1 (`win/winrt_blob.asm`, `.error`) — that concerns `madvise`, not the floor, but the PE side of any arena change is untested here.
- Whether any student has actually hit the lexer skip — there are no user reports in the tree; the harm is inferred from the mechanism, not measured.
- The exact error message `1e5` produces. The mechanism is clear (the lexer emits `1`, then the identifier `e5`; the parser then fails), but the exact parse-error text was not traced, and the reference documents only that it is refused (`:835`).
- Whether `patra_path_sarv`'s buffer-and-cap shape is actually hard for a student — there is no student feedback in the tree; that part of item 3 is a judgement.
- The current status of the 29-locals segfault beyond "not fixed" — no execution was allowed in this round, and no gate covers it; the R29 note is the last word.
- Whether the reference's `dvaram`/`paadh` rows are a deliberate shorthand rather than a swap. The code is unambiguous (`:22702`, `:22751`); the table's wording is the only evidence, and it says the opposite.
- The ranking itself is a judgement, not a reading — stated at the top of §4. In particular, item 3 (a teaching gap, not a compiler gap) and the 29-locals segfault (a crash, but rare) could be ordered the other way by a reader who weights severity over frequency; this round weights frequency, because every student who reaches files hits item 3 and almost no student writes a 29-local function.
- The `parimana()` `.zero`-path displacement question from R155/R158: still open, still needs execution; unrelated to this round, noted only so it is not mistaken for resolved.

